'''''Game of Thrones''''' is<!--Do NOT change to "was". It is and will always be a television series, hence "is" is correct, despite it having stopped airing.--> an American<!--Awards/nominations do not go here--> [[Fantasy television|fantasy]] [[Drama (film and television)|drama]]<!-- Do not add a flavor of fantasy, such as high, epic, or medieval fantasy here. Detailed discussion of genre, if it can be cited to reliable sources, belongs in the article body, not the lead sentence. --> television series created by [[David Benioff]] and {{nowrap|[[D. B. Weiss]]}} for [[HBO]]. It is the first adaptation of ''[[A Song of Ice and Fire]]'', a series of [[high fantasy]] novels by {{nowrap|[[George R. R. Martin]]}}, and the first in what would become the [[Game of Thrones (franchise)|''Game of Thrones'' franchise]]. The show premiered on HBO in the United States on April 17, 2011, and concluded on May 19, 2019, with 73 episodes broadcast over eight seasons.

Set on the fictional continents of [[Westeros]] and [[Essos]], ''Game of Thrones'' has a large [[ensemble cast]] and follows several [[story arc]]s throughout the course of the show. The first major arc concerns the [[Iron Throne (A Song of Ice and Fire)|Iron Throne]] of the [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire#Seven Kingdoms|Seven Kingdoms]] of Westeros through a web of political conflicts among the noble families, either vying to claim the throne or fighting for independence. The second major arc focuses on the last descendant of the realm's deposed ruling dynasty, who has been exiled to Essos and is plotting to return and reclaim the throne. The third follows the Night's Watch, a military order defending the realm against threats from beyond the Seven Kingdoms' northern border.

''Game of Thrones'' attracted a record viewership on HBO and has a broad, active, and international fan base. Many critics and publications have named the show one of the [[List of television shows considered the best|greatest television series of all time]]. Critics have praised the series for its acting, complex characters, story, scope, and production values, although its frequent use of [[Nudity in film|nudity]] and violence (including [[sexual violence]]) generated controversy. The [[Game of Thrones season 8|final season]] received significant criticism for its reduced length and creative decisions, with many considering it a disappointing conclusion.<!-- See the "critical reception" section for references. The lead is a summary and as such is normally not footnoted. --> The series received 59 [[Primetime Emmy Awards]], the most by a drama series, including [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Drama Series|Outstanding Drama Series]] in 2015, 2016, 2018 and 2019. Its [[List of awards and nominations received by Game of Thrones|other awards and nominations]] include three [[Hugo Award for Best Dramatic Presentation|Hugo Awards for Best Dramatic Presentation]], a [[Peabody Award]], and five nominations for the [[Golden Globe Award for Best Television Series – Drama]].

Two prequel series, ''[[House of the Dragon]]'' and ''[[A Knight of the Seven Kingdoms (TV series)|A Knight of the Seven Kingdoms]]'', premiered on HBO in 2022 and 2026, respectively. {{TOC limit|3}}

==Premise==
{{See also|A Song of Ice and Fire#Plot synopsis|l1=Synopsis of ''A Song of Ice and Fire''|World of A Song of Ice and Fire{{!}}World of ''A Song of Ice and Fire''}}
''Game of Thrones'' is roughly based on the storylines of the ''[[A Song of Ice and Fire]]'' book series by {{nowrap|[[George R. R. Martin]]}}, set in the fictional [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire#Westeros|Seven Kingdoms]] of [[Westeros]] and the continent of [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire#Essos|Essos]].<ref name="var">{{cite news |last=Fleming |first=Michael |title=HBO turns 'Fire' into fantasy series |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |publisher=[[Penske Media Corporation]] |date=January 16, 2007 |access-date=March 2, 2010 |url=https://variety.com/2007/scene/markets-festivals/hbo-turns-fire-into-fantasy-series-1117957532/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120516224747/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117957532?refCatId=14 |archive-date=May 16, 2012 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{cite book |last=Cogman |first=Bryan |title=Inside HBO's Game of Thrones |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gzYfBQAAQBAJ |access-date=November 6, 2016 |date=November 6, 2014 |publisher=[[Orion Publishing Group|Orion]] |isbn=978-1-4732-1040-0 |page=4 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161106185619/https://books.google.com/books?id=gzYfBQAAQBAJ |archive-date=November 6, 2016}}</ref> The series follows several simultaneous plotlines.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ordoña |first1=Michael |title='Game of Thrones': Second chances and second lives for key characters |url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment/envelope/la-en-st-game-of-thrones-character-arcs-emmys-20190516-story.html |access-date=May 22, 2020 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=May 16, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200105144206/https://www.latimes.com/entertainment/envelope/la-en-st-game-of-thrones-character-arcs-emmys-20190516-story.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=January 5, 2020}}</ref> The first [[story arc]] follows a [[war of succession]] among competing claimants for control of the [[Iron Throne (A Song of Ice and Fire)|Iron Throne]] of the Seven Kingdoms, with other noble families fighting for independence from the throne. The second concerns the actions of the exiled [[Lineal descendant|scion]] to reclaim the throne; the third chronicles the threat of the impending winter, as well as the [[Others (A Song of Ice and Fire)|legendary creatures]] and fierce peoples of the North.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/04/08/game-of-thrones-winter-is-coming-review |title=Game of Thrones: "Winter is Coming" Review |last=Fowler |first=Matt |date=April 8, 2011 |website=[[IGN]] |publisher=[[Ziff Davis]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120817073932/http://tv.ign.com/articles/116/1160215p1.html |archive-date=August 17, 2012 |access-date=January 20, 2022}}</ref>

=== Cast and characters ===
{{main|List of Game of Thrones characters|l1 = List of ''Game of Thrones'' characters}}

''Game of Thrones'' has an [[ensemble cast]] which has been estimated to be the largest on television.<ref name="hibberd1">{{cite magazine |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150105005619/http://insidetv.ew.com/2012/05/29/game-of-throne-season-3-cast/ |archive-date=January 5, 2015 |last=Hibberd |first=James |title='Game of Thrones' scoop: Season 3 character list revealed – Exclusive |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |publisher=[[Meredith Corporation]] |url=https://ew.com/article/2012/05/29/game-of-throne-season-3-cast/ |access-date=January 20, 2022 |date=May 29, 2012 |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2014, several actors' contracts were renegotiated to include a seventh-season option.<ref>{{cite news |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160813105026/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/game-thrones-cast-signs-season-744314 |archive-date=August 13, 2016 |last1=Belloni |first1=Matthew |last2=Goldberg |first2=Lesley |title='Game of Thrones' Cast Signs for Season 7 with Big Raises |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/game-thrones-cast-signs-season-744314/ |access-date=January 20, 2022 |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |publisher=[[Eldridge Industries#Media holdings|Valence Media]] |date=October 30, 2014 |url-status=live}}</ref> By the final season, five of the main cast members made {{nowrap|$1&nbsp;million}} per episode, making them among the [[List of highest paid American television stars|highest paid television performers of all time]].<ref>{{Cite magazine|last1=Gonzales|first1=Erica|last2=Jones|first2=Alexis|date=April 1, 2019|title=Game of Thrones Stars Could Be Making Millions Per Episode in the Final Season|magazine=[[Harper's Bazaar]]|publisher=[[Hearst Communications]]|url=https://www.harpersbazaar.com/culture/film-tv/a12048623/game-of-thrones-salaries-per-episode/|url-status=live|access-date=July 5, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190402041040/https://www.harpersbazaar.com/culture/film-tv/a12048623/game-of-thrones-salaries-per-episode/|archive-date=April 2, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Back|first=Grace|date=May 15, 2019|title=Here's How Much Money The 'Game of Thrones' Cast Makes Per Episode|work=[[Marie Claire#Australia|Marie Claire Australia]]|publisher=[[Pacific Magazines]]|url=https://www.marieclaire.com.au/game-of-thrones-salary|url-status=live|access-date=November 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190515155716/https://www.marieclaire.com.au/game-of-thrones-salary|archive-date=May 15, 2019}}</ref>

<!-- Only list characters from the main cast, who were credited in the opening credits, are included here; Myrcella, Renly, Oberyn, Jojen, Meera, Osha, the Mountain, etc should not be listed -->[[Ned Stark|Eddard "Ned" Stark]] ([[Sean Bean]]) is the head of [[List of A Song of Ice and Fire characters#House Stark|House Stark]]. With his wife [[Catelyn Stark|Catelyn]] ([[Michelle Fairley]]), he has children [[Robb Stark|Robb]] ([[Richard Madden]]), [[Sansa Stark|Sansa]] ([[Sophie Turner]]), [[Arya Stark|Arya]] ([[Maisie Williams]]), [[Bran Stark|Bran]] ([[Isaac Hempstead-Wright]]) and [[Rickon Stark|Rickon]] ([[Art Parkinson]]); he also has an [[Legitimacy (family law)|illegitimate]] son, [[Jon Snow (character)|Jon Snow]] ([[Kit Harington]]), who, along with his scholarly friend [[Samwell Tarly]] ([[John Bradley (English actor)|John Bradley]]), serves the [[Night's Watch]] under Lord Commander [[Jeor Mormont]] ([[James Cosmo]]). Wildlings living north of the Wall include [[Gilly (A Song of Ice and Fire)|Gilly]] ([[Hannah Murray]]), [[Tormund Giantsbane]] ([[Kristofer Hivju]]), and [[Ygritte]] ([[Rose Leslie]]).<ref name="telegraphcast">{{cite news |last1=Power |first1=Ed |title=Game of Thrones main characters and cast – a complete list of those alive and dead at the end of the finale |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/tv/0/game-thrones-cast-characters-full-list-alive-dead-finale/ |access-date=May 19, 2020 |work=[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]] |publisher=[[Telegraph Media Group]] |date=May 22, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190524194024/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/tv/0/game-thrones-cast-characters-full-list-alive-dead-finale/ |archive-date=May 24, 2019}}</ref>

Others associated with House Stark include Ned's ward [[Theon Greyjoy]] ([[Alfie Allen]]), Ned's [[vassal]] [[Roose Bolton]] ([[Michael McElhatton]]), and Bolton's illegitimate son, [[Ramsay Bolton|Ramsay Snow]] ([[Iwan Rheon]]). Robb allies with healer [[Talisa Maegyr]] ([[Oona Chaplin]]), while elsewhere, Arya befriends blacksmith apprentice [[Gendry]] ([[Joe Dempsie]]) and assassin [[Jaqen H'ghar]] ([[Tom Wlaschiha]]) and Catelyn is introduced to [[Brienne of Tarth]] ([[Gwendoline Christie]]).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Houbrick |first=Dan |date=February 21, 2019 |title=Game Of Thrones: A Breakdown Of House Alliances And Feuds |url=https://screenrant.com/game-thrones-house-feuds-alliances-explained/ |access-date=May 9, 2026 |website=ScreenRant |language=en}}</ref>

In [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire#King's Landing|King's Landing]], King [[Robert Baratheon|Robert I Baratheon]] ([[Mark Addy]]) shares a loveless political marriage with [[Cersei Lannister]] ([[Lena Headey]]). Her twin brother [[Jaime Lannister|Jaime]] ([[Nikolaj Coster-Waldau]]) serves on the Kingsguard while younger brother [[Tyrion Lannister|Tyrion]] ([[Peter Dinklage]]) is attended by mistress [[Shae (character)|Shae]] ([[Sibel Kekilli]]) and mercenary [[Bronn (character)|Bronn]] ([[Jerome Flynn]]). Cersei's father, [[Tywin Lannister|Tywin]] ([[Charles Dance]]), is the head of [[House Lannister]] and the richest man in [[Westeros]]. Among Cersei's children are sons [[Joffrey Baratheon|Joffrey]] ([[Jack Gleeson]]) and [[Tommen Baratheon|Tommen]] ([[Dean-Charles Chapman]]). Joffrey is guarded by the scar-faced warrior [[Sandor Clegane|Sandor "The Hound" Clegane]] ([[Rory McCann]]).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Alter |first1=Rebecca |last2=Polk |first2=Milan |title=The First and Last Appearances of Game of Thrones' Most Memorable Characters |url=https://www.vulture.com/2019/05/game-of-thrones-characters-first-last-appearances-deaths.html |access-date=May 19, 2020 |work=[[New York (magazine)#Digital expansion and destination sites|Vulture]] |publisher=[[Vox Media]] |date=May 22, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191212123722/https://www.vulture.com/2019/05/game-of-thrones-characters-first-last-appearances-deaths.html |archive-date=December 12, 2019}}</ref>

The king's Small Council includes treasurer [[Petyr Baelish|Petyr "Littlefinger" Baelish]] ([[Aidan Gillen]]) and [[spymaster]] [[Varys]] ([[Conleth Hill]]). On the island of [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire#Dragonstone|Dragonstone]], Robert's younger brother, [[Stannis Baratheon|Stannis]] ([[Stephen Dillane]]), is advised by foreign priestess [[Melisandre]] ([[Carice van Houten]]) and former smuggler [[Davos Seaworth]] ([[Liam Cunningham]]). In court, [[List of A Song of Ice and Fire characters#House Tyrell|House Tyrell]] is represented by [[Margaery Tyrell|Margaery]] ([[Natalie Dormer]]), her brother Loras ([[Finn Jones]]) and their grandmother [[Olenna Tyrell|Olenna]] ([[Diana Rigg]]). [[The High Sparrow]] ([[Jonathan Pryce]]) is given power as a religious leader while the warrior [[Ellaria Sand]] ([[Indira Varma]]) seeks vengeance against the [[Lannister]]s.<ref name="telegraphcast" />

Across the Narrow Sea, siblings [[List of A Song of Ice and Fire characters#Viserys Targaryen|Viserys]] ([[Harry Lloyd]]) and [[Daenerys Targaryen]] ([[Emilia Clarke]]) (colloquially referred to as "Dany") are in exile. Daenerys is forced to marry [[Khal Drogo]] ([[Jason Momoa]]), a leader of the nomadic [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire#Dothraki Sea|Dothraki]]. Her retinue comes to include exiled Westerosi knight [[Jorah Mormont]] ([[Iain Glen]]), aide [[Missandei]] ([[Nathalie Emmanuel]]), mercenary [[Daario Naharis]] ([[Ed Skrein]] and [[Michiel Huisman]]), and elite soldier [[List of Game of Thrones characters#Grey Worm|Grey Worm]] ([[Jacob Anderson]]).<ref name="telegraphcast" />

